(Ord. No. 2010-310, § 4, 1-27-2010)

369:
(a) Food trucks shall locate on privately owned lots zoned C-1 Neighborhood Commercial District, C-2 General Commercial District, and I-1 Industrial District, with all-weather surface (or gravel) parking areas, accessible by emergency vehicles, and having adequate legally designated off-street parking spaces, subject to the approval of the property owner and subject to the following:
(1) Written notice to the county health department seven days prior to operations beginning. Such notice shall state the expected dates of operation and shall specify the physical location of operations;
(2) The food truck shall be positioned at least 50 yards from the customer entrance of an existing restaurant during the food truck's hours of operation;
(3) No food truck shall be operated within 600 feet of any public school in the city limits between the hours of 7:30 a.m. and 3:30 p.m. on days in which schools are actually in session;
(4) The food truck shall be positioned on designated parking spaces and shall not block drive aisles, other access to loading/service areas, or emergency access and fire lanes;
(5) No food truck shall be located within a building setback or landscape buffer;
(6) The food truck must be positioned at least 25 feet away from fire hydrants, driveway entrances, alleys, handicapped parking spaces, tree trunks, and vegetation;
(7) Food trucks shall not be located within 100 feet of gas pumps, underground gas storage tanks, and vent pipes;
(8) Food trucks may not be located in any portion of a parking lot when and where such location would prevent the use of parking spaces during the regular hours of operation of the primary business on the lot, or otherwise interfere in a significant manner with the movement of motor vehicles, unless prior permission to use the parking spots has been provided by the property owner;
(9) No seating shall be provided unless approved by the city manager, building codes or police department;
(10) Vehicle-mounted roll-out awnings are allowed; and
(11) Vehicle must vacate premises by 10:00 p.m. and return to base of operation daily, unless permitted by set a number of days.
(b) Food trucks selling ice cream only shall be allowed to be in residential zoning districts subject to the following:
(1) All traffic laws must be observed and obeyed;
(2) Food truck selling ice cream only cannot block traffic or create traffic congestion;
(3) Food truck selling ice cream only can operate only between the hours of 9:00 a.m. and 8:00 p.m. on Monday through Saturday and no food truck selling ice cream only can operate in residential zoning districts at any time on Sunday;
(4) The noise level from the food truck selling ice cream only or any sound equipment attracting, or designed to attract, customers must comply with the noise ordinance and must not create a nuisance.
(Ord. No. 2021-399, § e, 10-8-2021)

418:
(a) Every pawnshop and pawnbroker shall maintain a permanent electronic record of all transactions so that an accurate description of all property pledged, traded or sold to the pawnshop or pawnbroker can be transmitted to the city police department via an electronic automated reporting system. Each transmission shall be made at the time of each transaction, and the following information shall be included therein:
(1) The date and time of the purchase, pawn or sale of property;
(2) The name, address and telephone number of the customer making the pledge, trade or sale;
(3) A description of the customer in terms of sex, race, date of birth, height and weight, as well as the driver's license number of the customer or some other identification card that contains a photograph of the customer;
(4) A description of the pledged or purchased property by serial, model or other number, if available, and by any identifying marks (e.g., brand name, color, style, etc.);
(5) The number of the receipt or pawn transaction issued for the property pawned or bought;
(6) The price paid or the amount loaned;
(7) The maturity date of the transaction, if a pawn;
(8) A photograph of the customer and of the item pawned or bought which will be taken with the electronic automated reporting system at the time of the transaction;
(9) The signature of the customer.
(b) Every pawnbroker and pawnshop shall upload electronically via the internet a batch file of all transactions for each business day to the electronic automated reporting system selected by the city immediately at the conclusion of each business day. The administrator of the electronic automated reporting system will electronically transmit all transactions to the city police department.
(c) In the event that the electronic automated reporting system becomes temporarily or permanently disabled, pawnbrokers and pawnshops will be notified as soon as possible. In this event, the pawnbroker and pawnshop will be required to make records of transactions in paper form. Such paper forms must include all information as enumerated in subsection (a) of this section. Pawnbrokers and pawnshops shall maintain a three-day supply of paper forms.
(d) Any duly authorized law enforcement officer may, during the ordinary hours of business or any other reasonable time, inspect any pawnbroker's electronic records at the pawnbroker's place of business to ensure compliance with this section.
(e) The chief of police or his designee shall select and designate the required automated reporting system. There will be a fee assessed to the pawnbroker for each reported transaction. Said fee shall be set from time to time by the mayor and council in such an amount that the anticipated aggregate fees collected will defray the cost of the city to maintain the automated reporting system. The fee shall be invoiced to the pawnbroker monthly by the vendor of the automated reporting system or as otherwise determined by the mayor and council.
(Ord. No. 2015-370, § 5, 9-8-2015)

420:
(a) Any person or entity that violates any provision of this article shall be subject to citation to the municipal court, and upon a determination by the municipal court that a violation of this article has occurred, shall be subject to a civil penalty not to exceed $1,000.00.
(b) In addition to the penalty set forth in subsection (a) of this section, a pawnbroker's registration shall be subject to suspension for due cause and after a hearing as provided in this article. The term "due cause," for the purposes of this article, shall include, but not be limited to, the pawnbroker or the pawnbroker's employee or agent:
(1) Making any false statement in the registration application provided for in section 10-280;
(2) Failing to maintain and make entries in the permanent record as required by section 10-281;
(3) Making any false entry in such permanent record;
(4) Falsifying, obliterating, destroying, or removing from the place of business such permanent record;
(5) Refusing to allow any duly authorized law enforcement officer to inspect such permanent record, or any precious metals or gems or goods made from precious metals or gems in his possession, during the ordinary hours of business or at any reasonable time;
(6) Selling, exchanging, or removing from the legal possession of the buyer, or altering the form of, any precious metals or gems or goods made from precious metals or gems purchased by remounting, melting, cutting up, or otherwise altering the original form until at least 14 calendar days have elapsed from the time of purchase or acquisition;
(7) Failing to make the report as required in this article;
(8) Purchasing any precious metals or gems from any person under 17 years of age;
(9) Conviction of the pawnbroker or any person holding an interest in the pawnbroker under any federal, state or local law of any felony or misdemeanor involving dishonesty or relating to precious metals; or
(10) Any other violation of this article.
(c) The city clerk shall notify the pawnbroker in writing by certified mail, return receipt requested, of any charge of conduct or offense subject to suspension of the registration. The notice shall also state the place and time the mayor and council will hear the charges against the pawnbroker. The notice may also contain such additional information, as the city clerk may deem appropriate. The notice shall be deposited into the United States mail at least five days prior to the hearing date.
(d) Unless waived by the pawnbroker, the mayor and council shall conduct a hearing on any charge against a pawnbroker alleging conduct or offense that is subject to a suspension at its next regularly scheduled meeting, but not later than 60 days from the date of notice to the pawnbroker unless a continuance is granted at the request of the city or the pawnbroker. The mayor and council shall have the discretion to call a special meeting to conduct a meeting or to reschedule or continue any hearing upon the request of the city or the pawnbroker. The pawnbroker shall be allowed to appear at said hearing and to present evidence and cross-examine witnesses. Upon hearing evidence from the city and the pawnbroker, if the pawnbroker shall present any evidence, the mayor and council shall, no later than 30 days after the hearing, either dismiss all or some the charges against the pawnbroker and/or, make a finding that a violation has occurred as to all or any one of the charges. If a violation is found to have occurred mayor and council shall levy a suspension of the registration as follows:
(1) First violation: 30-day suspension.
(2) Second violation within 12 months of previous violation: 60-day suspension.
(3) Third violation within a five-year period: One-year suspension.
(e) If due cause to assess sanctions is found to exist, then the pawnbroker shall be charged with and shall pay, in addition to any sanction, the actual costs of the court reporter arising from the take down and transcription of the hearing proceedings and, if applicable, the cost of translation services.
(f) Should the pawnbroker desire to appeal the decision of the public safety commission, the pawnbroker must do so by writ of certiorari directly to the county superior court within 30 days of the date of such decision of the mayor and council.
(Ord. No. 2015-370, § 7, 9-8-2015)

